# --- automation & IaC (from Debian where present; the rest via sysible-release) ---
 ansible, ansible-lint,
# sshpass is what lets Ansible use a PASSWORD over SSH at all. Without it
# `--ask-pass` / ansible_password does not degrade — it refuses outright with
# "to use the 'ssh' connection type with passwords, you must install the sshpass
# program", which is exactly the first run against a fresh host that has no key
# on it yet. A hard dep: the bootstrap run is the one that cannot go and fetch a
# missing package first.
 sshpass,
# --- containers ---
 podman, buildah, skopeo,
Recommends:
# Heavier or GUI pieces — installed by default, removable with --no-install-recommends.
 gnome-core, calamares,
# SysTerm terminal (published from its own repo; installed when available)
 systerm,
# desktop apps
 wireshark, virt-manager, virt-viewer,
# office suite — core apps + GNOME integration (the full `libreoffice` metapackage
# is much larger). Obsidian isn't in Debian; the vendor hook installs it.
 libreoffice-writer, libreoffice-calc, libreoffice-impress, libreoffice-gnome,
# VM guest integration (clipboard, display auto-resize, pointer alignment) —
# Sysible Workstation commonly runs in a VM, incl. on Apple Silicon. This needs BOTH
# families and the reason is not cosmetic.
#
# open-vm-tools is VMware's and does nothing on a KVM/QEMU host. On KVM with
# SPICE the guest pointer is RELATIVE without spice-vdagent, so the cursor the
# guest draws drifts away from the host's: you click where you see the arrow and
# the press lands somewhere else. That reads as "my clicks don't work".
#
# These were added to the ISO's package list in 1.0.3 and NOT here, so every
# machine installed from an earlier image kept the broken pointer no matter how
# many times it ran apt upgrade — the metapackage is the only thing that reaches
# an installed system. tests/test_guest_tools_cover_the_hypervisors_claimed.py
# now asserts this file and the ISO list together so that cannot recur.
 open-vm-tools, open-vm-tools-desktop,
 spice-vdagent,
# The KVM counterpart to open-vm-tools' host-side half: lets virsh shut the guest
# down cleanly, report its addresses (virsh domifaddr), and freeze filesystems for
# a snapshot. Inert on bare metal — the service finds no virtio channel and stays
# stopped — which is why it sits in Recommends alongside VMware's.
 qemu-guest-agent,
# languages (bigger toolchains)
 golang, rustc, cargo, nodejs, npm, default-jdk, maven, gradle,
# databases
 postgresql-client, default-mysql-client, sqlite3, redis-tools,
# security
 openssl, gnupg, fail2ban, auditd, apparmor, lynis,
# btop/atop/iftop live in contrib/backports on some suites — Recommends keeps them soft
 btop, atop, iftop,
# eza is Debian 13+; soft-depend so a bookworm build doesn't fail
 eza,
# CherryTree — GPL hierarchical note-taking app, in Debian (installs on amd64 and
# arm64). Replaces Obsidian (proprietary, not redistributable, no arm64 build).
 cherrytree,
# These come from third-party repos wired up by sysible-release; Recommends so a
# missing upstream repo never blocks the whole install:
 docker-ce, docker-compose-plugin, containerd.io,
 kubectl, helm, k9s,
 opentofu,
 awscli, sops, age,
 codium,
 gh,
Suggests:
 azure-cli, google-cloud-cli,
 sysible-controller, sysible-agent,
Description: Sysible Workstation — engineering workstation (all-in-one)
 Turns a Debian base into the Sysible Workstation engineering workstation: the tools a
 Linux/DevOps/SRE/platform engineer reaches for, installed and ready on first boot,
 so there is no half-hour of apt after every fresh install.
 .
 Depends carries the must-haves (shell, networking, containers via podman, the
 modern CLI set, Python, Ansible, the Sysible CLI and SysTerm terminal). Recommends
 carries the heavier and GUI pieces (GNOME, VSCodium, Docker CE, Kubernetes, OpenTofu,
 cloud CLIs, virtualization) so a lean build can drop them with --no-install-recommends.
 .
 Tools that are not in Debian (Docker CE, kubectl/helm, Terraform/OpenTofu, cloud
 CLIs, VS Code) are pulled from their upstream apt repositories, which sysible-release
 configures — so `apt install` Just Works without vendoring their binaries.
